11.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp leader transcript contains four regions that can form alternative RNA hairpins. The 2-3 antiterminator hairpin is mutually exclusive with the 3-4 terminator hairpin; formation of 2-3 therefore precludes termination. Region 1 normally competes with region 3 for pairing with region 2. A mutation that abolishes 1-2 pairing leaves region 2 free to base-pair with region 3 as soon as region 3 is transcribed. Consequently the 3-4 terminator cannot form, even when the ribosome is not stalled (high tryptophan). This produces read-through under repressing conditions—the phenotype requested.

Option B blocks the antiterminator and therefore favors, rather than prevents, 3-4 formation. Option C alters the poly-U tract downstream of the hairpin, not the stem-loop itself. Option D has no direct effect on hairpin choice, and E merely reduces initiation. Thus only A satisfies both mechanistic and phenotypic criteria.}

3.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp attenuator relies on mutually exclusive RNA hairpin structures in the trpL leader transcript. Under high tryptophan the ribosome translates through region 1 and covers region 2, freeing region 3 to base-pair with region 4 and form the rho-independent terminator. A mutation that prevents region 1 from pairing with region 2 leaves region 2 free to pair with region 3 as soon as region 3 is transcribed. Formation of the 2–3 antiterminator therefore sequesters region 3 and blocks the 3–4 terminator even when tryptophan is abundant, allowing read-through.  

B favors 3–4 pairing and therefore increases termination.  
C alters the poly-U tract downstream of the hairpin and would affect release rather than hairpin formation itself.  
D and E affect expression level but do not selectively disrupt the 3–4 stem-loop.  

Thus only choice A directly prevents terminator formation and permits continued transcription under repressing conditions.}

